\par\par \b \uc2  WRAPUP 2-Asia stocks mixed, eyes Fed action next week. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Edwin Chan \par \uc2 734 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Mai 2000\par 09:21\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, May 12 (Reuters) - Asia's stock markets turned in a mixed performance on Friday following solid gains on Wall Street, but investors were cautious ahead of a widely-anticipated rise in key U.S. interest rates next week. \par \par \uc2  "Sentiment is not too positive and people are quite cautious ahead of the Federal Open Market Committee meeting on Tuesday," said Alex Wong, research manager at OSK Securities in Hong Kong. \par \par \uc2  In \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, the yen was trading below its early intraday peak but was clinging to gains helped by a recovery in Japanese stock prices. \par \par \uc2  Tokyo's Nikkei average finished up 2.82 percent at 17,357.86 after four straight losing sessions. \par \par \uc2  Tokyo traders said overall sentiment was cautious because of potential volatility next week when the policy-setting Federal Open Market Committee meets. \par \par \uc2  Australia's S&P/ASX 200 index also staged a rebound following recent losses, closing 1.28 percent up at 3,034.8. \par \par \uc2  Hong Kong stocks were firmer following a losing streak since May 3. The Hang Seng Index was up 1.50 percent at 14,709.72 by 0705 GMT. \par \par \uc2  Wall Street rallied overnight after a government report showed U.S. retail sales fell 0.2 percent in April - the first decline in almost two years. \par \par \uc2  The closely watched data might ease pressure on the Fed to keep tightening interest rates in a bid to cool the economy. \par \par \uc2  Before the data, analysts forecast the Fed would hike U.S. rates by 50 to 75 basis points, even after the expected increase in rates next week. \par \par \uc2  Fed-watchers now turn to U.S. April Producer Price Index data due later on Friday, with the PPI expected to post a 0.2 percent drop and core-PPI - which strips out volatile food and energy prices - seen gaining 0.1 percent. \par \par \uc2  The Dow Jones Industrial Average rose 1.72 percent to end at 10,545.97 on Thursday, while the technology heavy Nasdaq rose 3.39 percent to 3,499.58 after enduring three consecutive sessions of steep losses. \par \par \uc2  Taiwan's TAIEX ended up 2.52 percent at 8,560.44 in cautious trade, while South Korea's KOSPI fell 2.53 percent to 740.30. \par \par \uc2  And Malaysia's Kuala Lumpur Composite index was off 0.76 percent at 913.72 in mid-afternoon trade. \par \par \uc2  TOKYO STOCK RALLY LIFTS YEN \par \par \uc2  The yen extended overnight gains against the dollar and the euro after Japanese stocks rebounded. \par \par \uc2  Ken Landon, a senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Deutsche Bank, said Wall Street's recovery had reduced concern about investors pulling money out of Japan to cover their U.S. positions. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar should stablise around 107 yen and again try the 110 level," Landon said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the dollar's retreat to a low of 107.65 yen was also driven by technical factors as many traders, caught with long positions after the dollar's quick decline from Thursday's high of 110.08 yen, sought to sell on rallies. \par \par \uc2  Heavy demand for yen in cross trade, particularly against the euro and sterling, was also weighing the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  down. \par \par \uc2  Dealers were unsure if the dollar's setback was a correction after the recent rally or a shift in the dollar's uptrend. \par \par \uc2  "It's really difficult to predict direction at the moment after it touched the long-targeted 110 yen," a trust bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  Around 0715 GMT, the dollar was quoted at 108.19/29 yen compared with 108.45 yen in U.S. trade late on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said a Fed rate rise of less than 50 basis points would hurt the dollar. \par \par \uc2  The euro was holding above 90 U.S. cents but had no clear direction in Tokyo after the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  held interest rates steady on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Around 0715 GMT, the euro was trading at $0.908/13 versus $0.9013 in late U.S. trade. \par \par \uc2  The euro/yen was at 97.51/59 against 97.71 yen in late U.S. trade. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was expected to remain under downward pressure after European officials failed to offer any indication they would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the ailing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  ((Edwin Chan, \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  Asiadesk +65 870-3822, fax +65 870-3820, email: eddie.chan@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 2000. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw5c00k3n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
