\par\par \b \uc2  Singapore Dlr Ends Up, Supported By Fear Of MAS Action \b0\par\par\uc2 300 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Mai 2000\par 11:51\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The Singapore dollar ended moderately stronger against the U.S. dollar on Friday as dealers hesitate to sell the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for fear of provoking \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Around 0935 GMT, the U.S. dollar was at S$1.7245, down from Thursday's close of S$1.7265. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar gained despite a steep drop in the Indonesian rupiah. South Asia's \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  often move in tandem. \par \par \uc2  The stability of the Singapore \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Friday was a testament to the effectiveness of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in support of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  While the MAS may allow the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken somewhat if the region's other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  continue to soften, it's not likely to allow the U.S. dollar to climb much above S$1.7350, said Patrick Bennett, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Warburg Dillon Read. \par \par \uc2  "I think if we go to S$1.7350 they will step in again," Bennett said. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar and other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are under pressure from expectations that the U.S. Federal Open Market Committee will raise its key interest rate by 50 basis points when it meets next week. \par \par \uc2  Although the rate hike appears to be inevitable, investors are now starting focus on the possibility that rates will continue to rise in the U.S. after next week's FOMC meeting. \par \par \uc2  As rates rise, higher yielding U.S. investments start to look more attractive. In addition, stock market volatility in the U.S. is prompting foreign investors to reduce positions in emerging markets. \par \par \uc2  In Singapore's local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  money market, the overnight rate was offered unchanged at 0.125%. The three-month rate was offered at 3.0625%, also unchanged. \par \par \uc2  1163 1859 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dw5c02crx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
