\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Under Pressure Late From Rupiah's Slide \b0\par\par\uc2 783 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Mai 2000\par 10:30\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The Indonesian rupiah continued to weaken Friday on a series of misstatements by senior policymakers, putting pressure on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in other Asian markets. \par \par \uc2  In late trading Friday, the U.S. dollar briefly surged past the level of IDR8,700, its highest point since Sept. 27, after the governor of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said he would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the rupiah only "if it will be effective." \par \par \uc2  The statement by Sjahril Sabirin was widely interpreted as a sign of weakness by traders already inclined to sell the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  following a statement on Thursday by Indonesia's top economic official, Kwik Kian Gie, who said he couldn't recommend his country to foreign investors. \par \par \uc2  The Thai baht and the Philippine peso were dragged lower by the rupiah. Regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  had already been weakened by expectations of an impending rise in interest rates in the U.S., where the Federal Open Market Committee is widely expected to hike its key federal funds rate by 50 basis points when it meets Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar, meanwhile, was higher in late trading Friday following reports that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to defend the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday. The Thai baht, while lower, also gained support from reports of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  the previous day. \par \par \uc2  Around 0845 GMT, the U.S. dollar was at IDR8,485, up from IDR8,375 late Thursday but off its intraday peak. \par \par \uc2  Against the baht, the U.S. dollar was at THB38.845, up from THB38.815 late Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar also rose to PHP41.475 from PHP41.430. The peso continued to suffer from the Muslim insurgency in the southern province of Mindanao, where rebels are holding foreign hostages, said Philip Wee, treasury economist at Standard Chartered Bank. \par \par \uc2  Among southeast Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , only the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar gained ground following Thursday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The U.S. dollar fell to S$1.7243 from Thursday's S$1.7265. \par \par \uc2  Traders hesitated to bid the U.S. dollar above the level of S$1.7300 for fear of provoking a further round of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the MAS, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  "If selling intensifies to the levels we have seen, I'm sure they will step back in," said Philip Wee, treasury economist at Standard Chartered Bank. \par \par \uc2  But across southeast Asia, \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are likely to weaken further on expectations that U.S. rates will continue to rise even after next week's Fed meeting. And a volatile U.S. stock market is adding to the uncertainty, prompting investors to withdraw funds from relatively risky emerging markets, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the supply of U.S. dollars had all but dried up in Indonesia, with exporters withholding the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the market in the expectation that the rupiah will fall even further. \par \par \uc2  Patrick Bennett, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Warburg Dillon Read, said the market is waiting for a stronger stance from the Indonesian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "They don't want to come in just to provide liquidity to the market," Bennett said. "Nothing that's being said is dissuading anyone from holding on to these dollars." The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , he said, "Needs to come out and say something stronger." \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the rupiah would continue to weaken as the market starts to wonder how long Kwik would stay in his job. Some observers said the dollar could quickly jump to IDR9,000 or beyond. \par \par \uc2  "The markets will remain very cautious in Indonesia," said David Simmonds, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Citibank in Singapore. "There clearly will continue to be talk and speculation about a further cabinet reshuffle." \par \par \uc2  Indonesian President Abdurrahman Wahid, however, said he had no plans to replace Kwik, calming the markets somewhat. He blamed the weakening \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the anniversary of 1998 ethnic riots, saying local businesses were holding on to their U.S. dollars for fear of unrest. \par \par \uc2  In South Korea, the U.S. dollar rose to KRW1,114.5 in late trading, up from KRW1,109.10 on Wednesday. Thursday was a holiday in Korea. Friday's closing level was the weakest for the won since April 17, when it touched KRW1,115.30 to the U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said, however, that the won was unusual among Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in that it would gain against the dollar if authorities tighten monetary policy in response to surging economic growth. \par \par \uc2  The Taiwan dollar got a boost from a stock market rally. The U.S. dollar ended trading at NT30.739, down from Thursday's close at NT30.752. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  also supported the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said. \par \par \uc2  -By Christopher Wellisz; (65) 421-4802; christopher.wellisz@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dw5c02cls\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
