\par\par \b \uc2  Singapore Dlr Steady Early, But Faces Continued Pressure \b0\par\par\uc2 195 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Mai 2000\par 04:39\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The Singapore dollar is steady early Friday, but dealers say the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  still faces continued pressure against the U.S. dollar due to the prospects for higher U.S. interest rates. \par \par \uc2  At 0220 GMT (10:20 p.m. EDT Thursday), the U.S. dollar was quoted at S$1.7265, unchanged from late Thursday. The pair are expected by dealers to trade in a range of S$1.7250-S$1.7290 Friday. \par \par \uc2  Analysts say the bias toward the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in global markets probably will continue at least through next week's Federal Open Market Committee meeting, at which policy makers are expected to raise short-term interest rates by a half percentage point. \par \par \uc2  Market players probably will be reluctant to push the U.S. dollar too high, dealers said, following reports that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The MAS allegedly stepped in after the U.S. dollar had risen to S$1.7351, its highest level in 13 months. \par \par \uc2  -By Alan Yonan Jr.; 65 421-4859; alan.yonan@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1163 1859 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dw5c02c47\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
