\par\par \b \uc2  Singapore Straits Times Index Ends Down 2.6% To 2003.72 \b0\par\par\uc2 646 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Mai 2000\par 11:03\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- Singapore shares closed sharply lower Thursday on a host of factors, pushing the Straits Times index below the 2000 points level for the second time since April 17. \par \par \uc2  The 5.6% decline in the Nasdaq and the 1.6% fall in the Dow Jones Industrial Average overnight were key triggers to the selling Thursday. The U.S. declines added to already strong worries about the impact of a sharply higher interest rate hike in the U.S., which is Singapore's key trading partner. \par \par \uc2  The Federal Reserve is set to revise the federal fund rate when its rate-setting Federal Open Market Committee meets May 16. Market participants expect a 50 basis point hike. \par \par \uc2  Also adding to market concerns was the Singapore dollar's sharp decline against the U.S. dollar Thursday - to S$1.7351, triggering an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar fell following a sharper decline in the Indonesian rupiah after Indonesian Senior Economics Minister Kwik Kian Gie told Dow Jones Newswires that Indonesia isn't yet stable enough politically to honestly invite foreign investors to invest. \par \par \uc2  The statement comes at a time when the Singapore government is encouraging local companies to invest in Indonesia. Some companies such as Cycle & Carriage have already done so. \par \par \uc2  The benchmark Straits Times Index fell 53.50 points, or 2.6%, to 2003.72. The DBS-50 index, which gives greater weighting to the largest stocks and excludes foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  issues, fell 16.34 points, or 2.5%, to 647.29. \par \par \uc2  The Straits Times index hit an intraday low of 1986.57 points - the lowest since April 17 when it sank to 1981.29 in intraday trading. \par \par \uc2  Volume was estimated at 548.5 million shares, compared with 488.4 million shares Wednesday. Decliners led advancers 280 to 64, with 102 stocks unchanged. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                       Thursday Previous  Change\par Straits Times Index   2003.72  2057.22   Dn 53.50\par DBS-50                 647.29  663.63    Dn 16.34\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par At the end of 1999, the benchmark Straits Times Index was at 2479.58\par points and the DBS-50 was at 822.28 points.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Singapore's decline wasn't an isolated event. Kwik's comments\par triggered a 3.4% decline in Jakarta stocks, while the Nasdaq's fall was\par blamed for the 4.6% fall in the Nikkei 225 index.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In Singapore, financial and technology stocks were the worst hit.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The finance subindex, which has been falling since worries about\par interest rates emerged last week, fell 3% to 1351.33. The electronics\par subindex shed 3.1% to 178.21.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par DBS Group Holdings, Southeast Asia's largest bank by assets, continued\par its downward trend Thursday. The stock shed 90 Singapore cents, or 4.3%,\par to S$20.20 on volume of 2.6 million shares. Overseas Union Bank was the\par second biggest loser in absolute terms, shedding 33 cents, or 4.6%, to\par S$6.85 on 3.7 million shares traded.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The list of top decliners Thursday was littered with big-time\par technology names.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Pacific Century Regional Developments, parent of Hong Kong's Pacific\par Century Cyberworks, fell 70 cents, or 5.2% to S$12.80 on volume of 1.5\par million shares.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par NatSteel Electronics shed 60 cents, or 6.8%, to S$8.20 on trade of 3.2\par million shares.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Singapore Telecom, which faced a Thursday deadline to seal a deal to\par buy into Malaysia's Time Engineering, shed 1 cent to S$2.33 on trade of\par 1.6 million shares. The companies are expected to separately make an\par announcement later Thursday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Singapore Airlines, which constitutes 7.27% of the Straits Times\par index, shed 80 cents, or 4.6%, to S$16.70 on 1.8 million shares.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Hasan Jafri; 65-421-4828; hasan.jafri@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  5012 55114 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dw5b02dfj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
