\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Down Late As Mkts Brace For US Rate Hike \b0\par\par\uc2  By Alan Yonan Jr. \par \uc2 683 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Mai 2000\par 11:33\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  slumped Wednesday as the dollar continued to build momentum ahead of next week's expected interest rate hike in the U.S. \par \par \uc2  Local factors, such as political uncertainty in Indonesia and a hostage crisis in the Philippines, also weighed on individual Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar fell to its lowest level in nearly a year, while the Indonesian rupiah and Thai baht tumbled to their lowest levels in roughly seven and five months, respectively. \par \par \uc2  The dollar held steady against the yen, remaining above a key Y109 support level. \par \par \uc2  The Federal Open Market Committee, the U.S. Federal Reserve's policy setting arm, is widely expected to boost short-term rates by a half a percentage point when it meets on May 16. \par \par \uc2  "Going forward, most regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  will weaken across the board as we move closer to the FOMC meeting," a dealer at a foreign bank said. \par \par \uc2  A recent spate of strong economic data in the U.S. has boosted market expectations that the Fed will have to tighten credit aggressively to keep the economy from overheating. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar dropped sharply in the first hour of regional trading and remained pressured throughout the day. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hit it's low point near the close of local trading when the U.S. dollar rose to S$1.7308, its highest level since May 27, 1999 when it touched $1.7317. \par \par \uc2  Near the end of regional trading, the U.S. dollar slipped back to S$1.7301, up from S$1.7250 late Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Such levels in the past would have surely triggered \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But analysts say recent weakness in the yen and euro means the MAS probably has developed a higher tolerance for U.S. dollar appreciation against the Singapore \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The rupiah also wilted under prospects of higher U.S. rates. Additionally, the slide was exacerbated by lingering uncertainty about the government's ability to bring about economic reform. \par \par \uc2  Late Wednesday the dollar was quoted at 8,203 rupiah, up from IDR8,070 late Tuesday. Wednesday's closing level was the highest for the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  since Oct. 15, 1999, when it reached IDR8,260 in intraday trading. \par \par \uc2  In Manila, concern over a hostage crisis and fighting between government and Muslim rebel forces in the southern Philippine region of Mindanao added to the gloomy sentiment for the peso. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at 41.370 pesos on the Philippine Dealing System, up from PHP41.279 late Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Traders said relatively thin demand for dollars and some banks taking profits on recent gains in the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  helped relieve pressure on the peso. \par \par \uc2  The Thai baht ended lower as dollar selling by local exporters failed to offset a surge in the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Late in the day the dollar rose to 38.955 baht, the highest level since it touched THB38.975 on Dec. 1, 1999. The baht edged back at the close, with the dollar quoted at THB38.915, up from THB38.530 late Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The New Taiwan dollar moved lower amid concerns over rising tensions with mainland China ahead of the inauguration of president-elect Chen Shui-bian on May 20. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar closed at NT$30.697, compared with NT$30.690 Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The South Korean won ended slightly higher following another session of narrow range-bound trading, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar finished at 1,109.10 won, down slightly from Tuesday's close at KRW1,109.40. \par \par \uc2  Dollar selling by local exporters kept pressure on the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . In the afternoon, state-run Korea Development Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market after the dollar hit an intraday low of KRW1,108.70, keeping the dollar above the KRW1,109 level for the remainder of the session. \par \par \uc2  Seoul financial markets will be closed Thursday for a holiday. Trading will resume Friday. \par \par \uc2  -By Alan Yonan Jr. 65 421-4859; alan.yonan@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dw5a02bph\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
