\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Mixed Late In Holiday-Thinned Trading \b0\par\par\uc2  By Benjamin Pedley \par \uc2 674 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Mai 2000\par 10:17\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are mixed against the U.S. dollar late Friday in trade thinned by holidays in Japan, South Korea and Thailand. \par \par \uc2  The dollar took a break from its recent rise against the yen, giving support to some regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Late in the local trading session it fell toward Y107 after peaking earlier this week at a two month-high of Y109.61. \par \par \uc2  A decision overnight by the Bank of England's monetary policy committee to leave interest rates at 6% triggered heavy sales of sterling, particularly against yen, which helped keep the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  firm during the Asian session. \par \par \uc2  Most traders, however, were on the sidelines awaiting the issue tonight of U.S. non-farm payrolls data for clues on the likely scale of interest rate action by the Federal Reserve. \par \par \uc2  Further evidence of a tighter labor market would fuel expectations of aggressive monetary tightening, and likely push the dollar higher against Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Expectations for the jobs report center on a 310,000 rise in employment in April after a 416,000 rise in March, and a fall in the jobless rate to 4.0% from 4.1%. \par \par \uc2  The federal funds rate is 6% now, but analysts expect it will be lifted to 7%, or more, by year-end to keep inflation in check. Most expect the Fed's policy-making arm, the Federal Open Market Committee, to hike the fed funds rate 50 basis points when it next meets May 16. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar, which often tracks the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Japan - a key trading partner - rebounded with the yen after posting a nine-week low Thursday against the U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  Late in the day, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was quoted at S$1.7190, down from S$1.7240 a day earlier. \par \par \uc2  "It's tracking the dollar-yen movement," a dealer at a U.S. brokerage said. "But trading is pretty listless with a lot of traders out for the holidays," he added. \par \par \uc2  Speculation the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the local unit around S$1.7250 - as traders say it did in recent months - also helped it rise. \par \par \uc2  The Thai baht, however, failed to benefit from the strengthening yen in offshore trade. \par \par \uc2  Foreign speculators took advantage of low baht liquidity, due to the holidays in Japan and Thailand, to buy dollars in the hope of making profits in the short-term. \par \par \uc2  Thai Deputy Finance Minister Pisit Leeahtan attempted to soothe jittery financial markets, saying the baht's weakness likely will be only temporary. \par \par \uc2  Pisit, speaking to reporters on the sidelines of an Asia Development Bank annual meeting in Chiang Mai, said the baht's decline Thursday to a five-month intraday low against the dollar at THB38.560 was "something of a surprise." \par \par \uc2  Exports and other economic indicators are doing well, meaning the baht is out of kilter with fundamentals, he said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was at 38.455 baht from THB38.425 a day earlier. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, the New Taiwan dollar rose slightly, helped by a 3.3% jump in local stock prices. The U.S. dollar was quoted at NT$30.648, compared with NT$30.699 at the previous close. \par \par \uc2  The Indonesian rupiah and Philippine peso were barely changed. \par \par \uc2  Late in the day the dollar was at 7,967 rupiah, down from IDR7,975 late Thursday, and unchanged at 41.280 pesos. \par \par \uc2  Philippine \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Rafael Buenaventura said the monetary board next week will "most likely" leave the overnight borrowing rate unchanged at 9% after inflation came in at lower-than-expected 3.7% year-on-year in April. \par \par \uc2  However, a 50-basis-point hike in the U.S. may force a response from Philippine monetary officials for fear of sharp decline in the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The South Korean won didn't trade because of a government holiday. The dollar ended Thursday at 1,111.0 won. \par \par \uc2  -By Benjamin Pedley; 65-421-4485; ben.pedley@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dw55028m5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
