\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2 : Stock Sell-Off Weighs On Won, S$ Rises \b0\par\par\uc2  By Alan Yonan Jr. \par \uc2 711 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 April 2000\par 11:22\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The South Korean won tumbled under the weight of a stock market sell-off Friday, while the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar spiked up on reports of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by local monetary authorities. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere in the region, \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were lower in light trading. \par \par \uc2  South Korea's Kospi index plunged 4.7% Friday as stocks across Asia moved lower in the wake of Wall Street's recent downturn. \par \par \uc2  The won fell to its lowest level in more than a week as foreign investors pulled their money out of stocks, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Opinion was divided over what impact Thursday's general elections in South Korea would have on the market. Some analysts said the ruling party's failure to secure a majority could potentially slow the pace of reform in corporate restructuring. Others, however, said the fallout would be minimal. \par \par \uc2  Late Friday, the dollar was quoted at 1,111.00 won, up from KRW1,108.00 Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar jumped to its highest level in nearly two weeks, continuing a rally that began Thursday. A number of dealers said they believed the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore got things started by entering the market and selling dollars at about the S$1.7195 level Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Players who had taken long U.S. dollar positions were forced to unload the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , pushing it to an intraday low of S$1.7108 Friday. That was the lowest level since it was S$1.7103 on April 3. \par \par \uc2  "I doubt anyone will go long with the U.S. dollar now," a dealer at a foreign bank said. "The MAS is sending a message." \par \par \uc2  By the close, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moved to S$1.7115, down from S$1.7152 late Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Some analysts said the timing of the supposed MAS \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was puzzling, since the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar didn't appear to be unusually soft. \par \par \uc2  But there also was speculation that the MAS did it as a preemptive move to head off what it may have perceived as a trend toward a weaker Singapore dollar. The timing was calculated, the theory goes, to take advantage of the long U.S. dollar positions. \par \par \uc2  The New Taiwan dollar also felt the pressure of declining share prices. The island's main index fell 2.98%, and has now dropped 7.4% over the past four sessions. \par \par \uc2  Dealers also said sentiment toward Taiwan stocks and the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  are still being negatively affected by tensions with mainland China. \par \par \uc2  Late in Asia, the U.S dollar was quoted at NT$30.461, up from NT$30.380 at the end of the previous session. \par \par \uc2  The Indonesian rupiah remained in relatively good shape one day after the government reached an agreement with the Paris Club group of creditor nations to reschedule $5.8 billion worth of sovereign debt. \par \par \uc2  The debt rescheduling, almost $2.2 billion of which is slated to take place this year, is contingent upon the successful completion of an International Monetary Fund review by June 5 at the latest, an IMF official said. \par \par \uc2  The IMF is optimistic it can resume lending to Indonesia by late-May, after delaying its latest $400 million loan disbursement over Indonesia 's failure to keep pace with its reform program. \par \par \uc2  At the close of trading in Jakarta, the dollar was at IDR7,623, up from IDR7,600 late Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The Philippine peso moved lower in thin trading amid corporate demand for U.S. dollars. Declines were limited, however, following a favorable assessment of the Philippine economy by the IMF. \par \par \uc2  The fund said Thursday it was confident that the Philippines will be able to meet its growth target of four to five percent this year and set the stage for "rapid growth" in the next five years. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was quoted at 41.168 pesos at the close of trading, up from 41.155 the previous session. \par \par \uc2  The Thai baht lost some ground in light offshore trading as markets in Bangkok were closed for a public holiday. Dealers said the baht selling was typical before a weekend. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at THB37.965, up from THB37.900 late Thursday. \par \par \uc2  -By Alan Yonan Jr. 65-421-4859; \par \par \uc2  alan.yonan@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dw4e01x10\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
