\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Mostly Higher Late In Tandem With Yen \b0\par\par\uc2  By Benjamin Pedley \par \uc2 605 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 April 2000\par 10:57\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- A more upbeat assessment of the Japanese economy by the Bank of Japan and comments by BOJ Governor Masaru Hayami that he wants to end a zero-interest rate policy "as soon as possible" pushed most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  higher late Wednesday in line with a surging yen. \par \par \uc2  Late in the day, the dollar was quoted at Y105.50, down sharply from Y106.66 a day earlier and Y106.99 late Tuesday in New York. \par \par \uc2  Brighter economic prospects for Japan augur well for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of its regional trading partners, as demand for their exports is likely to rise. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ in its April report card on the economy said improvement "is becoming distinct" on a rosier outlook for business investment. It added that recovery has started in some areas of private demand. \par \par \uc2  This was a notch up from the BOJ's previous report, in which it said the economy had "started to improve" but clear signs of a self-sustained recovery in private demand were yet to be seen. \par \par \uc2  Yen strength pushed the Singapore dollar, the South Korean won, the New Taiwan dollar, and the Thai baht higher late Wednesday. The Philippine peso was lower, but off its intraday lows on the late afternoon comment from Hayami. \par \par \uc2  On the eve of an important South Korean parliamentary election, the won rose slightly as foreign capital inflows into the local stock market offset dollar-buying by the state-run Korea Development Bank. \par \par \uc2  Financial markets hope incumbent President Kim Dae-jung will retain enough parliamentary support to push through further economic reforms, but Kim's Millennium Democratic Party faces the challenge of maintaining control of 273-seat assembly as recent opinion polls have it trailing by about 10 seats. \par \par \uc2  However, with up to 40% of voters still undecided, many analysts agree the first ever summit between the two Koreas, announced Monday, tips the scale toward the ruling party. \par \par \uc2  The dollar ended at 1,108.0 won, down from KRW1,109.4 previously. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere in North Asia, the New Taiwan dollar rose on heavy buying by exporters to end at NT$30.303 from NT$30.307 late Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar, which weakened steadily this week on planned foreign acquisitions by Singapore companies such as Singapore Airlines and Singapore Telecommunications, rose slightly, helped also by expectations the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  if it falls further. \par \par \uc2  Traders say the MAS \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  April 5 around the S$1.7225 level. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was at S$1.7180 late, down from S$1.7205 a day earlier. \par \par \uc2  The baht was also a touch higher late, buoyed by a 24% jump in Thai exports to $5.90 billion from a year earlier, and 51.2% on-year growth in auto sales in the first quarter. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was at 38.020 baht, down from THB38.040 a day earlier. \par \par \uc2  The Indonesian rupiah, however, ended unchanged at 7,620 rupiah against the dollar as traders await the outcome of a meeting Wednesday and Thursday of the Paris Club of creditors. Financial markets expect it will agree to roll over $2.1 billion of Indonesian sovereign debt maturing this year. \par \par \uc2  In Manila, the dollar rose to 41.175 pesos from PHP41.120 as local banks appeared to test a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  pledge to allow market forces to determine the exchange rate. Traders suspect the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the peso if it trades below PHP41.200. \par \par \uc2  -By Benjamin Pedley; 65 421 4485; ben.pedley@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dw4c01w1q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
