\par\par \b \uc2  Singapore Dlr Down Late Morning In Range-Bound Trade \b0\par\par\uc2 244 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 April 2000\par 05:34\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The Singapore dollar is lower against the U.S. dollar at late morning with trading remaining in a fairly narrow range. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hovered around the S$1.7200 level and market participants apparently were reluctant to push it much further on fears that monetary authorities would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore last week successfully beat back the U.S. dollar after it rose to a near-term high of S$1.7240, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  At 0310 GMT (11:10 p.m. EDT Tuesday) the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was quoted at $1.7200, up from S$1.7197 late Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The pair were expected to trade in a range of S$1.7150 to $1.7200, with a first level of resistance expected at S$1.7250, dealers said. Trading in recent weeks generally has been limited to a S$1.7100 to S$1.7200 range. \par \par \uc2  Some analysts say recent pressure on the Singapore \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was triggered by a slew of foreign acquisitions by local companies, who need to buy U.S. dollars to finance the deals. Others, however, say \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  commitments for such acquisitions are probably worked through the market in advance, and therefore have minimal impact on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Alan Yonan Jr. 65-421-4859; alan.yonan@dowjones.com\par -0- 12/04/00 03-34G\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1