\par\par \b \uc2  Singapore Dollar Sapped By Investments In Foreign Assets \b0\par\par\uc2  By Christopher Wellisz \par \uc2 689 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 April 2000\par 12:22\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- What's good for companies like Singapore Airlines Ltd. (P.SAL) is bad for the Singapore dollar - at least in the short run. \par \par \uc2  Singapore Airlines shares rose 3.3% Tuesday on news that it had bought an 8.3% stake in Air New Zealand for NZ$141.3 million (NZ$1=US$0.4974). \par \par \uc2  But the Singapore dollar weakened on expectations that Singapore Airlines and other local corporations would have to sell the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to finance foreign acquisitions. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore Airlines deal is the latest in a string of acquisitions by cash-rich local companies seeking to expand beyond the tight confines of the island-state. \par \par \uc2  In recent months, Singapore Airlines paid GBP600.25 million for 49% of Virgin Atlantic Ltd., and Cycle & Carriage Ltd. (P.CYC) took 23% of Indonesian auto maker PT Astra International (P.ASI) for US$310 million. \par \par \uc2  Such news "does have an impact on the Singapore dollar just now," said David Simmonds, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Citibank. "There are likely to be further outflows relating to international mergers and acquisitions." \par \par \uc2  Late Tuesday, the U.S. dollar was trading at S$1.7197, up from S$1.7165 late Monday. Many \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers say the U.S. dollar would be stronger still if not for the threat of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  News of the deals has pushed down a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  that just a few months ago had been expected rise sharply on the back of Singapore's robust economic growth. Some analysts had predicted that the local dollar would strengthen to S$1.55 to the U.S. dollar by year's end. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par        More Outflows Expected On More M&As\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par More money yet is expected to flow out of the country, creating a\par demand for U.S. dollars and other foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Singapore Airlines is in talks to buy another 16.7% of Air New Zealand\par from its principal shareholder, Brierley Investments.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Singapore Telecommunications Ltd. (P.SGT) is in talks to buy stakes in\par Malaysia's Time Engineering and two of its subsidiaries in a deal\par estimated to be worth 2.1 billion ringgit (US$1=MYR3.80).\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par And on Tuesday, Indonesia's Bank Restructuring Agency said it had\par talked to the Government of Singapore Investment Corporation about\par taking part in the planned initial share offering of PT Bank Central\par Asia (P.PCA).\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Chia Hsin-Li, Asian markets strategist at J.P. Morgan, says the market\par tends to overreact to news of foreign investments.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In some cases, like that of Singapore Airlines, the company may\par already have a stock of U.S. dollars, so the transaction doesn't move\par through the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market. In others, companies quietly\par accumulate foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for months before an announcement is made.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Even so, the overall effect is to weaken the Singapore dollar,\par analysts say, because the investments create demand for foreign\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par There are other reasons for the Singapore dollar's weakness, of\par course. One is the strength of the U.S. dollar against the euro and the\par yen, which lifts the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  against the local dollar as well.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Another is the low level of interest rates in Singapore, making the\par Singapore dollar a favorite funding \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for plays on other regional\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets, like South Korea.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Analysts add that the Singapore dollar is a tightly managed \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ,\par and that the MAS guides it within a band against a trade-weighted basket\par of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , including the dollar, euro and yen.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Ultimately, the level of the Sing is determined by monetary policy\par imperatives," said Simon Flint, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  economist at the Bank of\par America.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par On the one hand, the MAS wants to maintains the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at a level\par that keeps exports competitive. But on the other hand, it wants to avoid\par imported inflation through an overly weak \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par At the moment, most analysts say, there's little incentive for the MAS\par to tighten monetary policy because inflation is well under control at an\par annual rate of 1.3%.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Christopher Wellisz; 65/ 421-4802;\par christopher.wellisz@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dw4b01uk8\par }\page {\par\