\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Mixed Late; Singapore Dlr Slides On M&A \b0\par\par\uc2  By Benjamin Pedley \par \uc2 611 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 April 2000\par 09:30\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are mixed late Tuesday as the spotlight remains on the yen, which continues to weaken against the dollar and the euro ahead of a weekend meeting of finance ministers and central bankers from the Group of Seven leading industrialized nations. \par \par \uc2  Traders expect the G7 will again express shared concern about a strong yen, and perhaps the weak euro, but with joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  extremely unlikely, the impact on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets may be minimal. \par \par \uc2  But for now, the weaker yen, along with planned foreign acquisitions by Singapore companies, pushed down the Singapore dollar toward levels where traders say the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  last week. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was at S$1.7205 late, up from S$1.7165 a day earlier, and near the recent MAS \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  level of S$1.7225. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  companies often need to sell the local dollar in the spot \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to finance foreign acquisitions. \par \par \uc2  National carrier Singapore Airlines Ltd. (P.SAL) confirmed Tuesday it has bought an 8.3% stake in Air New Zealand (A.AIZ) for an undisclosed amount, and remains in negotiations with Brierley Investments Ltd.(A.BRY), along with Qantas Airways Ltd. (A.QAN), to purchase a further 17% of the carrier. \par \par \uc2  Singapore Telecommunications Ltd. (P.SGT), or SingTel, recently detailed plans to buy a stake in Time Engineering Bhd.(P.TEN) of Malaysia for more than 2.2 billion ringgit ($1=MYR3.80), while the Government of Singapore Investment Corporation (GIC) - the government's investment arm - may be considering taking a "significant stake" in Bank Central Asia (P.PCA) of Indonesia through an initial public offering. \par \par \uc2  Singapore's Business Times newspaper reported the Indonesian Bank Restructuring Agency approached GIC about taking a stake. \par \par \uc2  None of the parties referred to were immediately available for comment. \par \par \uc2  IBRA owns 92.8% of BCA, and wants to sell 30% of the bank in an IPO to raise around US$200 million. \par \par \uc2  In neighboring Indonesia, the rupiah continued to recover from a six-month low last week amid market optimism that the Paris Club of creditors will agree to roll over $2.1 billion sovereign debt maturing this year. \par \par \uc2  The International Monetary Fund's endorsement Monday of the debt restructure, and an Indonesian newspaper report Tuesday that quoted Senior Economics Minister Kwik Kian Gie saying Japan has pledged to roll over its $1.6 billion share of the debt buoyed investor sentiment. Paris Club creditors and Indonesian officials will meet Wednesday and Thursday in Paris. \par \par \uc2  The dollar late in Asian trading was quoted against the rupiah at 7,615 rupiah, down a touch from IDR7,660 late Monday. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, the Thai baht was lower as market participants squared positions before markets close Thursday and Friday for the Songkran Festival. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was at 38.040 baht from THB38.000 a day earlier. \par \par \uc2  In Manila, the peso dropped to 41.120 pesos against the dollar from PHP41.100 previously. Traders cited ongoing question marks over President Joseph Estrada's leadership, and a fall in the baht for the peso fall. \par \par \uc2  In North Asia, the dollar ended flat against the won at 1,109.4, and a touch higher against the New Taiwan dollar at NT$30.307 from NT$30.300 after the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of China, Taiwan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , sold the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  aggressively in the domestic \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  lifted the dollar from an intraday low of NT$30.265, a new 30-month high for the Taiwanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  -By Benjamin Pedley; 65 421 4485; ben.pedley@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dw4b01u7n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
