\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Mixed; Singapore Dlr Up After MAS Action \b0\par\par\uc2  By Tom Holland \par \uc2 668 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 April 2000\par 10:30\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  HONG KONG -(Dow Jones)- Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  had a mixed day Thursday. With regional markets in Thailand and Indonesia closed for holidays, activity was muted. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar ended local dealings significantly higher, after the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market during London trading hours Wednesday, selling U.S. dollars in support of the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , according to dealers. \par \par \uc2  Over the preceding days market players in Singapore had built short positions in the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in anticipation of MAS \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to maintain the exchange rate within its intended trading band. \par \par \uc2  Those positions became the target of speculative traders in London, who pushed the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  higher hoping to cause a cascade of U.S. dollar purchases, as stop-loss orders to liquidate their short positions were triggered. \par \par \uc2  The attempt was partially successful, driving the dollar up to levels around S$1.7225 Wednesday in London before the MAS entered the market. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  pushed the U.S. dollar rapidly below S$1.7150, a level from which the exchange rate barely deviated through Asian trading Thursday, as market participants remained nervous of provoking further official action. \par \par \uc2  Dealers didn't rule out a further effort to trip stop-loss orders above the S$1.7200 level, although they said a second attempt was unlikely in the immediate short term. \par \par \uc2  Late Thursday in Asia the U.S. dollar was quoted at S$1.7156, down from S$1.7185 at the same time the day before. \par \par \uc2  Other Southeast Asian markets were quiet Thursday. Fear of official \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the Philippine peso kept the dollar below its six-month high of PHP41.200. \par \par \uc2  At the close of local trading the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was quoted against the peso at PHP41.120, down from PHP41.130 the previous day. \par \par \uc2  With markets in Jakarta and Bangkok closed for local holidays, offshore volumes in the rupiah and the baht were extremely thin. \par \par \uc2  Sentiment toward the rupiah remained poor, however, after the International Monetary Fund last week postponed the disbursement of its latest tranche of assistance funding. The delay has raised market fears that Indonesia may fail to clinch an agreement at its April 12 meeting with the Paris Club of sovereign creditors. \par \par \uc2  Analysts at Standard Chartered Bank warned the Indonesian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains vulnerable after falling to its lowest level since mid-October on Wednesday, when the dollar was bid up to hit IDR7,800. \par \par \uc2  Analysts at Bank of America added that a deal on debt restructuring is urgently needed, together with a resumption of lending to Indonesia. \par \par \uc2  "The rupiah is likely to retain a weak tone in the short term, due to consistent corporate demands for dollars to repay debts," they wrote in a research note, which cautioned that the dollar may rise to IDR8,000 "earlier than expected." \par \par \uc2  Near the end of Asian hours the dollar was quoted in the offshore market at IDR7,785, unchanged from late the previous day. \par \par \uc2  Against the baht the dollar was slightly higher at THB38.135, up from THB37.995 the day before. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar is still looking bid," commented one broker. \par \par \uc2  In North Asia the New Taiwan dollar ended a touch lower as foreign investors unloaded local stocks, partly in response to technology sector volatility and partly on fears that the government is to begin to liquidate its holdings of local equities. \par \par \uc2  At the close the U.S. dollar was at NT$30.469, up marginally from NT$30.465 the day before. \par \par \uc2  The South Korean won gained ground, however, with the dollar easing to close at KRW1,112.00, down from KRW1,113.70 Tuesday. The Seoul market was closed Wednesday for a holiday. \par \par \uc2  -By Tom Holland; 852 2832 2341; tom.holland@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  SUBSCRIBERS CAN FIND ASIAN \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  COMMENT ON: \par \par \uc2  TELERATE PAGES [1136] OR [4250] \par \par \uc2  DOW JONES NEWSWIRES BY SEARCHING THE CODE N/FXW \par \par \uc2  BLOOMBERG BY ENTERING NI FRX \par \par \uc2  REUTERS BY ENTERING CODE \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2 /DJ \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dw4601run\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
