\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Mixed,  \highlight22\uc2 Singapore \highlight\uc2  Dlr Up On  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 486 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 April 2000\par 03:48\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 SINGAPORE\b0 \highlight\uc2  -(Dow Jones)- Most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are lower in early trading against the U.S. dollar Thursday, except for the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar, which is higher in the wake of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the local \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  entered the market Wednesday in London and New York, dealers said, after a decline in the Singapore dollar triggered stop loss orders that threatened to push the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to its lowest level for the year. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which had fallen during Asian trading hours, continued its decline in London as market participants looked to cover their short positions in U.S. dollars. Once the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rose to S$1.7189 the stop loss orders were triggered and the U.S. unit quickly jumped to S$1.7225, prompting the MAS to step in, a dealer at a U.S. brokerage said. \par \par \uc2  "They didn't want the Singapore dollar to hit a new low for the year based on technical selling," he said. \par \par \uc2  At 0130 GMT (9:30 p.m. EDT Wednesday) the U.S. dollar was quoted at S$1.7145, down from S$1.7185 late Wednesday in Asia. \par \par \uc2  A dealer at a foreign bank said he expected the pair to trade in a range of S$1.7135 to S$1.7180, with concerns over further MAS \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  limiting the topside. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, the Thai baht and Indonesian rupiah were lower in offshore trading as markets in the two countries were closed for holidays. The Philippine peso and Korean won also were lower. \par \par \uc2  Continuing concerns that Indonesia may not be able to meet International Monetary Fund deadlines for reform ahead of a key Paris Club meeting of creditors weighed on the rupiah, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was quoted at 7,790 rupiah, up from IDR7,785 late Wednesday. Dealers said they expected little activity in the rupiah trading Thursday because of the market closure. \par \par \uc2  The Philippine peso edged lower amid market anxiety over President Estrada's leadership. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was quoted at 41.155 pesos, up from PHP41.130 late Wednesday. Market participants may be reluctant to push the dollar much higher in the near term because of concerns the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  might \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market, a dealer at a foreign bank said. \par \par \uc2  In North Asia, the Korean won slipped from levels two days ago. Markets were closed in Korea Wednesday for a holiday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was quoted at 1,113.6 won, up from KRW1,113.4 late Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The New Taiwan dollar was slightly higher. The U.S. dollar was quoted at NT$30.471, down from NT$30.45 late Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Markets were closed in Thailand for a holiday, but the baht slid lower in offshore trading. The dollar was quoted at THB38.075, up from THB37.995 late Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  -By Alan Yonan Jr.; 65-421-4859 \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dw4601r5t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
