\par\par \b \uc2  Singapore Dlr Down Midmorning; Astra Sale,Indus Output Data \b0\par\par\uc2 268 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2000\par 05:21\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The Singapore dollar is lower against its U.S. counterpart midmorning Monday on news that a Singapore-led consortium will take a controlling stake in Indonesian automaker PT Astra International. \par \par \uc2  Weaker-than-expected data on industrial output, announced Sunday, also weakened the Singapore dollar, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  Around 0310 GMT (10:10 p.m. EST Sunday), the U.S. dollar was at S$1.7180, up from S$1.7149 late Friday. \par \par \uc2  The Indonesian government announced late Friday that a consortium led by Singapore's Cycle & Carriage Ltd. will take a stake of nearly 40% in Astra for US$506 million. \par \par \uc2  Capital outflows associated with the transaction have weakened the Singapore \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , analysts said. Some suggested that a portion of the total sum remains to be exchanged into U.S. dollars, meaning the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could weaken further. \par \par \uc2  Industrial data for February also sapped the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Manufacturing output rose at an annual rate of 9.4% in February, down from 28.2% in January. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said, however, the Singapore dollar is likely to be supported by the threat of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The MAS is concerned about volatility on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and also worries that a persistently weak Singapore dollar could spark inflation, said Simon Flint, an economist at the Bank of America in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  Philip Wee, treasury economist at Standard Chartered Bank in Singapore, said the U.S. dollar was unlikely to rise above S$1.7200. \par \par \uc2  -By Christopher Wellisz; 65/ 421-4802; christopher.wellisz@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1163 1859 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dw3r01kje\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
