\par\par \b \uc2  Singapore Dlr Edges Lower Midmorning, Maintains Firm Tone \b0\par\par\uc2 221 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2000\par 04:03\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The Singapore dollar is lower against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  midmorning Friday, although its tone remains firm following a recent round of U.S. dollar selling. \par \par \uc2  Around 0230 GMT (9:30 p.m. EST Thursday), the U.S. dollar was quoted at S$1.7094, up from S$1.7080 around 0858 GMT Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore unit started out the week on the defensive, trading around S$1.7230, with market expectations that it would test upward in the S$1.7300 to S$1.7400 range. \par \par \uc2  But that scenario failed to materialize, and the Singapore dollar strengthened as the week progressed. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  enjoyed a firm tone for most of the week as market participants unwound long U.S. dollar positions against the backdrop of a brighter yen. \par \par \uc2  A dealer at a U.S. brokerage said the U.S. dollar seems to have found support in the S$1.7080 to S$1.7090 range. If the dollar should fall through to the S$1.7000 level, however, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be prepared to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in an attempt to maintain an orderly ebb and flow of the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , traders said. \par \par \uc2  -By Alan Yonan Jr. 65-421-4859; alan.yonan@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1163 1859 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dw3a01av7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
