\par\par \b \uc2  Singapore Dlr Gains As Banks Unwind Long US Dlr Positions \b0\par\par\uc2 264 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2000\par 10:10\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The Singapore dollar ended stronger Thursday as U.S.-based merchant banks unwound long U.S.-dollar positions, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Around 0858 GMT (3:58 a.m. EST), the U.S. dollar was at S$1.7080, down from S$1.7178 late Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar selling started Wednesday, when one investment bank unloaded US$300 million, a larger order at a time when most players were betting that the U.S. unit would rise, a dealer at a European bank said. \par \par \uc2  On Thursday, "quite a lot of people were getting rid of long (U.S. dollar) positions around S$1.7150," the dealer said. \par \par \uc2  A large order to sell U.S. dollars is "still out there," another dealer said, suggesting that the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will continue to decline against the Singapore dollar. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said, however, that the U.S. dollar should find support at the current level of S$1.7080. But if that doesn't hold, the U.S. dollar will move toward S$1.7000, a level where the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  is thought likely to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar was consistent with gains in the Thai baht, Indonesian rupiah and other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Thursday. \par \par \uc2  In the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  money market, the overnight rate was offered at 0.8125%, down from 1.125% late Wednesday. The three-month rate was offered unchanged at 2.250%. \par \par \uc2  -By Christopher Wellisz; 65/ 421-4802; christopher.wellisz@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1163 1859 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dw3901a1g\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
