\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Under Pressure; Won Falls To Six-Wk Low \b0\par\par\uc2  By Tom Holland \par \uc2 753 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Februar 2000\par 09:30\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  HONG KONG -(Dow Jones)- Most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  came under pressure on Thursday as the recent weakness of the yen combined with souring sentiment toward local markets to drive regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  lower against the U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  Only the New Taiwan dollar held its own, as the Taiwanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stepped into the market to support the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , anxious to iron out any unwelcome volatility following the upsurge in political tensions with mainland China. \par \par \uc2  In Seoul, the won continued to weaken in response to the yen's slide and a deterioration in South Korea's trade balance. \par \par \uc2  "The yen is an important factor," said Steven Xu, treasury economist at Standard Chartered Bank in Hong Kong. "Korea targets its exchange rate on a cross-basis against the yen, and the long-term target is KRW10." \par \par \uc2  With the dollar/yen exchange rate testing five-month highs, the won has come uncomfortably close to that level recently. Only in the last few trading sessions has the Korean \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  begun in earnest to play catch up with the yen's fall. \par \par \uc2  Thursday saw the dollar trade as high as KRW1,141.00 against the won, its highest level in six weeks. According to traders, much of the dollar-buying was executed by Korean oil exporters, underlining fears that the current high crude oil prices will damage the trade balance of Korea, which is heavily dependent on energy imports. \par \par \uc2  Last week, South Korea announced a surprise $1.4 billion trade deficit in the first two weeks of February, sharply souring investor sentiment. Despite official reassurances that the country will still record a surplus for the month as a whole, that shock partly accounts for a selloff in local blue-chip stocks, which dragged down the main board Kospi index by 1.5% on Thursday, in turn weighing on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as foreign investors retreated. \par \par \uc2  At the close of Seoul trading Thursday, the dollar was quoted at KRW1,140.50, up from KRW1,135.30 the previous day. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere in North Asia, the New Taiwan dollar ended unchanged following purchases of the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Analysts said that despite the heightened tensions with Beijing, foreign investors in Taiwan were mostly standing firm, reassured by the Taiwan government's pledge to support local stock prices and by the structural strength of Taiwan's economy. \par \par \uc2  At the close, the U.S. dollar was flat against the New Taiwan dollar at NT$30.785. \par \par \uc2  In Southeast Asia, the Singapore dollar ended local trading lower after a large customer purchase of U.S. dollars against the Singapore \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was worked through the market during European hours on Wednesday, propelling the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  up through the S$1.7100 level. \par \par \uc2  Pointing to the current spate of foreign investments by Singapore companies, including a $240 million Hong Kong property purchase on Thursday by Singapore's Pidemco, dealers said the U.S. dollar is likely to test higher against the Singapore \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  over the short term. \par \par \uc2  "The MAS isn't in the market, so it could test S$1.7200," said one trader at a local bank, referring to the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which typically \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to maintain the exchange rate within an undisclosed target range. \par \par \uc2  Late Thursday in Asia, the U.S. dollar was quoted at S$1.7134, up from S$1.7083 early in European trading the previous day. \par \par \uc2  The Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  also lost ground, after baht liquidity in the offshore market eased following a funding squeeze on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Late in Asia, the dollar was quoted against the baht at THB38.190, up from THB38.030 the day before. \par \par \uc2  The rupiah came under selling pressure late in the session as fears over the frail health of Indonesian President Abdurrahman Wahid unsettled local markets, after the head of state canceled his schedule of meetings Thursday. Toward the end of local trading, the dollar was quoted against the rupiah at IDR7,885, up from IDR7,445 late Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The Philippine peso suffered in line with the regional trend and a downturn in the Manila stock market. At the close, the dollar was at PHP40.870, up from PHP40.700 the day before. \par \par \uc2  -By Tom Holland; 852 2832 2341; tom.holland@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  SUBSCRIBERS CAN FIND ASIAN \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  COMMENT ON: \par \par \uc2  TELERATE PAGES [1136] OR [4250] \par \par \uc2  DOW JONES NEWSWIRES BY SEARCHING THE CODE N/FXW \par \par \uc2  BLOOMBERG BY ENTERING NI FRX \par \par \uc2  REUTERS BY ENTERING CODE \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2 /DJ \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dw2o00zmx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
