\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Mostly Down Late; Won Makes 27-Mo High \b0\par\par\uc2  By Benjamin Pedley \par \uc2 711 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Februar 2000\par 09:52\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were mostly lower against the U.S. dollar late Friday, although the South Korean won bucked the trend to end sharply higher as funds continued to pour into the Korean stock market. \par \par \uc2  But overall, it was a day of U.S. dollar strength fueled by another record close on the technology-laden Nasdaq Composite Index. \par \par \uc2  The Nasdaq Composite Index jumped 2.8% Thursday to a new all-time high close of 4,485.63. This was the second record in the past three sessions, and it helped the dollar retain its overnight gains against the yen and euro. \par \par \uc2  The South Korean won ended trading in Seoul at its highest level to the dollar in nearly 27 months as foreign investors purchased won to pay for recent and future purchases in the local stock market. \par \par \uc2  The dollar finished at KRW1,115.30 won - its lowest close since Nov. 26, 1997 - down from KRW1,120.80 a day earlier. \par \par \uc2  Foreign investors bought a net KRW142.4 billion of Korean shares Friday, lifting their net purchases for the week to KRW822.53 billion. \par \par \uc2  The heavy dollar-selling pressure overwhelmed periodic attempts by state-run Korean Development Bank to shore up the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Although a decision Thursday by the Bank of Korea to raise its one-day call rate by 25 basis points triggered a round of won buying, analysts say it may eventually reduce upward pressure on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Morgan Stanley Dean Witter in a report said the rate hike will limit upward pressure on the won because the government has used its recent appreciation to stem inflationary pressure from rising import prices. \par \par \uc2  Traders said they expect the dollar to continue sinking next week as foreign funds that poured into the Seoul stock market late this week enter the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  The New Taiwan dollar ended little changed against its U.S. counterpart despite a rise in local share prices to a near 10-year closing high, and unexpectedly strong exports data. \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Taiwan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , in which it bought U.S. dollars, prevented the local dollar from rising. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of China wants to keep the New Taiwan dollar as weak as possible to ensure that its exporters remain competitive. \par \par \uc2  At the end of trading in Taipei, the dollar was at NT$30.613, little changed from late Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Trading volumes in other regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were down as Tokyo markets were closed Friday for a Japanese holiday. \par \par \uc2  In late trading in Jakarta, the dollar was quoted at IDR7,300, up marginally from IDR7,295 late Thursday as traders locked in profits on long rupiah positions. \par \par \uc2  Positive political and corporate developments, particularly a shakeup in the management of Indonesian automaker Astra in preparation for its sale, helped fuel the rupiah's recent rise from IDR7,700. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, the Singapore dollar was down against its U.S. counterpart, but well off its intraday low on news that Singapore Telecommunications Ltd. (P.SGT) may face competition in its bid to merge with Cable & Wireless HKT (HKT). \par \par \uc2  Pacific Century CyberWorks Ltd. (H.PCW) said it is considering investing in the unit of Cable & Wireless PLC (CWP). \par \par \uc2  When Singtel announced its merger plan, the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell as traders expected the company would need to sell Singapore dollars and buy sterling to pay Cable & Wireless for some of its stake in the Hong Kong company. \par \par \uc2  Traders expect the weakness in the Singapore dollar will continue to be limited to S$1.7000. They say the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  likely will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  at that level to curb the slide of the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . MAS bought Singapore dollars last week between S$1.7000 and S$1.7100. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar late Friday was trading at S$1.6935, up from S$1.6912 late Thursday, but below an intraday high of S$1.6977. \par \par \uc2  Against the Thai baht, the U.S. dollar was trading at THB37.475, up from THB37.365 a day earlier, while the dollar ended trading in Manila at PHP40.450, up from PHP40.385 late Thursday. \par \par \uc2  -By Benjamin Pedley; 65-421-4485; ben.pedley@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dw2b00t4c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
