\par\par \b \uc2  Singapore Dollar Weakens Sharply Late On Selling By Funds \b0\par\par\uc2 270 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Januar 2000\par 10:41\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The Singapore dollar dropped sharply in late afternoon trading as European funds sold the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for U.S. dollars, traders said. \par \par \uc2  About 0918 GMT (4:18 a.m. EST), the U.S. dollar was at S$1.6738, up from S$1.6684 late Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Traders were surprised by the sudden late afternoon move, which saw the U.S. dollar break out of a range of S$1.6710-S$1.6720 in which it had been trading for most of the day. \par \par \uc2  "There's a lot of interest to buy," said a dealer at a European bank. "Everybody's on the same side." \par \par \uc2  The dealer said there was only one major seller, a French institution. \par \par \uc2  The sharp move on the Singapore dollar was out of step with other regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which were generally stable against the U.S. dollar. Usually, regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  move in tandem. \par \par \uc2  Some traders said the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy U.S. dollars, as they had done twice earlier in the week, but others said there was no clear evidence of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar could weaken further in the course of the European trading day, in large part because participants generally don't want to hold short U.S. dollar positions over the weekend. \par \par \uc2  In the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  money market, the overnight rate was offered at 0.500%, up from 0.125% late Thursday. The three-month rate was offered at 2.375%, unchanged. \par \par \uc2  -By Christopher Wellisz; 65/ 421-4802; christopher.wellisz@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1163 1859 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dw1e006ky\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
