\par\par \b \uc2  ANALYSIS - Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention \highlight\uc2 , old habits die hard. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Anchalee Koetsawang \par \uc2 908 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Januar 2000\par 04:47\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 SINGAPORE\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Jan 12 (Reuters) - Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets are facing more \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  this year, with many governments unwilling to leave domestic \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  values to the forces of supply and demand despite signs of solid economic recovery. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  analysts say that in light of rising inflationary pressures such \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may cause more problems than it resolves, and question whether in the long run it can be successful in stopping \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  gains that reflect economic fundamentals. \par \par \uc2  Recent \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  by South Korea and Taiwan show the reluctance of some Asian governments to stay away from the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, analysts say. \par \par \uc2  "They are likely to continue with their King Canute policy," said Marshall Gittler, head of the global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk group at Bank of America in Hong Kong, referring to a Viking king of old who thought he was powerful enough to hold back the tide. \par \par \uc2  OLD HABITS DIE HARD...IF THEY DIE AT ALL \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Korea jumped into the market on the first trading day of 2000 to rein in the won as it surged to its highest level in two years on the back of a strong tide of capital inflows. \par \par \uc2  It has been curbing the won's rise although most analysts believe the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is undervalued. The latest Citibank forecast said the won should rise to 1,050 per dollar by the second quarter and 1,000 in the fourth. \par \par \uc2  Dealers are on a sustained won \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  vigil, especially after President Kim Dae-jung said in a New Year message that Korea's usable foreign reserves would rise to $100 billion by the end of 2000, around 35 percent up on their end-1999 level. \par \par \uc2  IT'S NOT JUST SOUTH KOREA \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of China, Taiwan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , was also in the market last week to curb the local dollar's rise. \par \par \uc2  And there were rumours the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore, one of the region's most efficient economies, stepped in to stem the domestic \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's gains. \par \par \uc2  There was no official denial of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  but Prime Minister Goh Chok Tong was quoted as saying Singapore has never depended on a weak \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stay competitive. \par \par \uc2  JAPAN LEADS \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  BINGE \par \par \uc2  The most glaring example of government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Asia would of course be the Bank of Japan, which has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets some 14 times since June to check the rising yen. \par \par \uc2  The most recent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on January 4 - Japan's first trading day of the new year - took the dollar from around 101.46 yen to highs of around 103.17 yen. \par \par \uc2  The dollar has since strengthened to above 105 yen, taking some heat out of the yen's extended rise, at least for now. \par \par \uc2  Japanese finance officials issue almost daily warnings about the economic risks of an excessively strong yen, but analysts have increasingly begun to question their arguments - as well as their success rate. \par \par \uc2  Given that dollar/yen once rose above 160 in April 1990 and fell as low as 79.70 in May 1995, last year's 101-125 range looks fairly stable. \par \par \uc2  And if the strong yen merely reflects a higher assessment of Japan's economy, it should not be causing such concern. \par \par \uc2  Deutsche Bank argues Japanese macroeconomic variables have historically been strongest in years following a yen rise, which is in some ways equivalent to a tax cut. \par \par \uc2  "Japanese corporate profits are decidedly better when the yen is strong," the bank said in a report. \par \par \uc2  "Corporate profits on average rose by 11 percent during \par \par \uc2   years when the yen strengthened against the dollar...and fell \par \par \uc2   by five percent in years when the yen was weak," it said. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  COULD WORK, BUT... \par \par \uc2  Analysts said Asian governments' desire to control their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets was not entirely unjustified, given that the aim was to stave off volatility and support the key exports sector. \par \par \uc2  But sometimes the line between effective and useless \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could be very thin. Badly executed \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  can distort the market and raise false expectations. \par \par \uc2  "In theory, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  does have some benefits. \b \highlight22\uc2 Currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  tend to overshoot and companies cannot handle such volatility," said Supavud Saicheua, head of economics and strategies at Merrill Lynch in Bangkok. \par \par \uc2  But he added that governments might not know where best to draw that line in the sand. \par \par \uc2  "Before the crisis, the Bank of Thailand thought the baht should be at 25 to the dollar and kept defending it at that level," he said. \par \par \uc2  "The result was disastrous." \par \par \uc2  SOMETHING HAS TO GIVE \par \par \uc2  The bottom line is that Asian governments cannot expect to have the best of all worlds: a strong economic recovery and subsequent capital inflows do not gel with a policy of keeping the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  soft and interest rates low. \par \par \uc2  To deliberately keep \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  weak to boost exports might not be a viable option at this juncture and may actually be unnecessary, given that much depends on global demand. \par \par \uc2  "\b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  can work short-term," said Chan Chia Lin, head of research at ABN AMRO Bank in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  "It can give you the best of both worlds - weak \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and low interest rates. But given the vigour of the recovery, it might not be viable," she said. \par \par \uc2  "Export demand is being driven more by the economic recovery and global demand rather than having competitive \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." \par \par \uc2  ((Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  Desk (65) 870-3090, fax:870-3787, email:asia.\b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 @reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 2000. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw1c02apj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
