\par\par \b \uc2  Malaysia PM: Govt May Take Over Clob Shares If Necessary \b0\par\par\uc2 531 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Dezember 1999\par 02:55\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  KUALA LUMPUR -(Dow Jones)- Malaysian Prime Minister Mahathir Mohamad said Thursday the government will take over the frozen Malaysian shares which once traded in Singapore, if the issue is not resolved. \par \par \uc2  "If necessary, we'll take over the shares," Mahathir said, adding that the government may also extend the nominee status given to Singapore's Central Depository Pte Ltd. \par \par \uc2  "If the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  takes over the Clob shares, the shares will still be owned by the investors," Mahathir said. \par \par \uc2  "We will have to redeem or compensate them for the shares," he added. \par \par \uc2  The Malaysian shares, estimated to be worth about 17 billion ringgit ($1=MYR3.80), were previously traded over the counter on Singapore's Central Limit Order Book, or CLOB, system, until Malaysia banned trading in these stocks as part of the capital controls it imposed in September 1998. \par \par \uc2  But Mahathir added that the decision to extend the deadline depends on the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  He said that Clob shareholders "should be happy" to accept the many "reasonable offers", while blaming Clob shareholders for battering the Kuala Lumpur stock market during the financial crisis. \par \par \uc2  Last week, a newly registered Singapore company, Collective Custodial Services, proposed taking custody of the Clob shares and then selling them over a 12-month period on the KLSE. The company will charge a 2% fee, the reports said. \par \par \uc2  It is the first such proposal from a Singapore company to end the deadlock. \par \par \uc2  Other proposals from Malaysian parties have involved selling the shares to them at heavily discounted prices. \par \par \uc2  Mahathir added that the Clob shareholders "have been dilly-dallying". \par \par \uc2  "I think that is not right," Mahathir said, adding that the Clob shares have appreciated by about 200% since September 1998. \par \par \uc2  Mahathir declined to comment on the remarks Tuesday by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  that it hopes the Malaysian government won't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the fate of the Clob shares. \par \par \uc2  Mahathir said the Malaysian stock market should continue to rise next year as the business climate is favorable and companies are doing well. \par \par \uc2  As such, he said the key stock index may hit 1000 points as early as next year. \par \par \uc2  Wednesday, the benchmark Kuala Lumpur Composite Index closed at 804.74 points. \par \par \uc2  Mahathir also said the Malaysian economy is expected to grow more than 4.0% this year. \par \par \uc2  In October, the government raised the 1999 official GDP forecast to 4.3% from 1.0% earlier. \par \par \uc2  "Next year should be better," Mahathir said, adding the economy has been insulated from short-term capital flows with the capital control measures in place. \par \par \uc2  "\b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders can no longer fiddle around with the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he said. \par \par \uc2  When Malaysia imposed capital controls in September 1998, it prohibited offshore trading of ringgit, effectively pegging the Malaysian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at MYR3.80 to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  The policy was crafted to allow Malaysia's economic planners to pump more liquidity into the recession-hit economy without undermining the stability of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The ringgit can now only be exchanged on the current account for\par trade-related purposes.\par  -By Netty Ismail; 603 292 5254; netty.ismail@dowjones.com\par -0- 30/12/99 03-00G\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dvcu08o20\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
