\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Lifted By Investor Buying In Thin Mkts \b0\par\par\uc2  By Tom Holland \par \uc2 683 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Dezember 1999\par 09:31\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  HONG KONG -(Dow Jones)- Most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  strengthened Thursday, lifted by moderate capital inflows in conditions of thin market liquidity. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar, however, ended Asian trading hours lower after customers bought U.S. dollars to hedge their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  exposure ahead of the year's end. \par \par \uc2  Government threats of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to slow the won's rise and aggressive dollar-buying by state-owned banks once again failed to damp buoyant market sentiment toward the South Korean \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Despite a warning from the Finance and Economy Ministry saying it's prepared to act to check the won's appreciation, along with sizable dollar purchases executed by the Korea Development Bank and the Korea Exchange Bank, investors chose rather to focus on the positive news to emerge from Seoul. \par \par \uc2  Boosting won sentiment, the Korea Development Institute said it expects GDP to expand by 13% year-on-year in the fourth quarter bringing the growth rate for 1999 as a whole to 10.1%. \par \par \uc2  For 2000, the KDI raised its forecast economic growth rate to a robust 7.8%, from 5.8% previously. \par \par \uc2  The institute also predicted that the inflation rate will rise to 3.2% next year from 0.8% this year, reinforcing market beliefs that the authorities will allow the won to appreciate in order to damp inflationary pressure, in preference to raising interest rates. \par \par \uc2  Won bulls were further encouraged by news that foreign direct investment has hit a record $15.2 billion so far this year, while the completion of the sale of Korea First Bank to U.S. investors, although not expected directly to affect the exchange rate, was also seen as a positive sign. \par \par \uc2  By the close of local trading, selling pressure had pushed the dollar down to KRW1,130.70 from KRW1,131.50 the previous day, despite the South Korean government's efforts to support the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Investor buying also lifted the New Taiwan dollar on Thursday, as offshore portfolio managers chased Taiwanese technology stocks following the rise of the U.S. Nasdaq Composite stock index to yet another record high. \par \par \uc2  An surge of selling hammered the U.S. dollar early in the session, prompting the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to enter the market to buy dollars in order to support the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Even so, the U.S. dollar still finished sharply lower, ending at NT$31.626, down from NT$31.662 at Wednesday's close. \par \par \uc2  In Southeast Asia, the Philippine peso rose strongly lifted by year-end remittances from expatriate Filipinos and confirmation that the Development Bank of Singapore has acquired a $700 million stake in Bank of the Philippine Islands. \par \par \uc2  By the close of trading in Manila, the dollar had slumped to PHP40.190, from PHP40.405 the previous day. \par \par \uc2  The Thai baht also strengthened, with the dollar slipping to THB37.42 late in Asian hours, down from THB37.53 late on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar, however, ended weaker after dropping sharply in afternoon trading. \par \par \uc2  The abruptness of the fall, which saw the U.S. dollar rise to S$1.6700 from levels around S$1.6650 within a matter of minutes prompted speculation that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  was \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market through domestic banks to weaken the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The idea was dismissed by one Singapore bank trader, who attributed the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's sudden dip to commercial hedging ahead of the year's end. \par \par \uc2  "Around S$1.66 is a good level to hedge," he said, adding that market illiquidity had exaggerated the extent of the move. "It only takes $50 million to move the market at the moment," he explained. \par \par \uc2  Toward the end of Asian dealing, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was quoted at S$1.6695, up from S$1.6660 late the day before. \par \par \uc2  The rupiah ended Asian hours almost untraded. Late in Asia the dollar was quoted against the Indonesian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at IDR7,080 compared with IDR7,095 the previous session. \par \par \uc2  -By Tom Holland; 852 2832 2341; tom.holland@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dvcn08jtr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
