\par\par \b \uc2  Singapore Dlr Up Early On Nov Export Data, Weak US Dlr \b0\par\par\uc2 200 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Dezember 1999\par 02:55\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The Singapore dollar is higher early Tuesday on strong export data and U.S. dollar weakness against the euro and yen. \par \par \uc2  At about 0147 GMT (8:47 p.m. Monday EST), the U.S. dollar was at S$1.6705, down from S$1.6730 late Monday. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  held steady Monday after the release of data showing that exports rose at an unexpectedly strong annual rate of 24.5% in November. \par \par \uc2  But the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  gained during European trading hours later Monday. "It seems like London banks are going long Asia," a dealer in Singapore said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said they don't expect the U.S. dollar to fall much below S$1.6700 in the course of the day. But they added \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  is unlikely, given the country's strong economic fundamentals. \par \par \uc2  "With the numbers being so good I don't see them coming in at all," said a dealer at a U.S. bank in Singapore. "I think they are quite happy at this level." \par \par \uc2  -By Christopher Wellisz; 65/ 421-4802; christopher.wellisz@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1163 1859 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dvcl08jnh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
