\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Mixed But Market Retains Bullish Outlook \b0\par\par\uc2  By Tom Holland \par \uc2 608 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Dezember 1999\par 09:30\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  HONG KONG -(Dow Jones)- Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  had a mixed day Friday, but most market players reaffirmed their bullish view of regional markets. \par \par \uc2  In North Asia the South Korean won closed just fractionally higher despite actual and verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by local authorities. \par \par \uc2  In Southeast Asia, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar nudged upward on portfolio inflows, extending gains made over the previous two days. \par \par \uc2  The Thai baht, however, closed marginally lower as profit-taking pared its recent gains. \par \par \uc2  The Indonesian rupiah closed flat, while the Philippine peso and the New Taiwan dollar both lost ground. \par \par \uc2  In Seoul, the Finance and Economy Ministry issued what is fast-becoming its customary early morning threat to act to suppress an appreciating won. \par \par \uc2  "The government stance that won's rise is never desirable to the country's economic recovery is unchanged," the ministry said in a statement. "The government will take proper measures anytime." \par \par \uc2  But other news Friday from South Korea only served to reinforce the market's impression that ultimately the authorities are fighting a losing battle in their attempts to check the won's rise. \par \par \uc2  Data released Friday showed that South Korean corporate bankruptcies fell in November to their lowest level since June 1991. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, government officials confirmed that the country currently has no need to draw on further funds available under the International Monetary Fund's 1997 rescue package. \par \par \uc2  "South Korea is seeing a lot of flows coming in, but the market remains wary of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Personally I don't see a rapid (dollar) move to KRW1,100 in the near term," said Syetarn Hansakul, regional treasury economist at West LB in Singapore, forecasting that the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will remain locked in a KRW1,125 to KRW1,135 range until the year's end. \par \par \uc2  At Friday's close the dollar was at KRW1,130.30, down slightly from KRW1,131.30 Thursday, with light purchases from state-run banks early in the session. \par \par \uc2  In Southeast Asia the Singapore dollar strengthened on portfolio inflows to the local stock market, a trend that is likely to accelerate in 2000, according to Thio Chin Loo, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Banque Paribas in the island republic. \par \par \uc2  Traders and investors have long remained wary of buying into the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar too aggressively, lest they trigger \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has been keen to guard the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's competitiveness. \par \par \uc2  But with economic growth rates robust and the global electronics cycle swinging upward, the authority's stance may change in 2000, argued Thio. \par \par \uc2  "The MAS is very likely to tolerate a stronger Singapore dollar in the New Year," she said. \par \par \uc2  Late Friday in Asia, the U.S. dollar was quoted against the Singapore \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at S$1.6725, down from S$1.6775 the previous day. \par \par \uc2  Profit-taking saw the Thai baht ease from the four-month high set late Thursday, but dealers reported continued interest to buy the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from smart money investors attempting to front-run January's anticipated portfolio inflows. \par \par \uc2  Late in Asia, the dollar was quoted at THB38.0200, up from THB37.9150 the previous day. \par \par \uc2  The Indonesian rupiah closed flat at IDR7,065. \par \par \uc2  In Manila the Philippine peso softened, with the dollar advancing to close at PHP40.635, up from PHP40.615 Thursday. \par \par \uc2  In Taipei, the New Taiwan dollar also eased, as foreign investors booked profits ahead of the year's end. At the close the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was at NT$31.657, up from NT$31.647 at the end of Thursday's session. \par \par \uc2  -By Tom Holland; 852 2832 2341; tom.holland@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dvch08e5c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
